              "instructions": "Read the passage and then answer the questions below. /6x1=6 points/",
              "passageTitle": "Counting Birds for Science",
              "text": "Every February, hundreds of thousands of ordinary people around the world spend fifteen minutes doing something unusual: they count birds. They might stand in a park, look out of a kitchen window or walk along a beach. Then they write down every bird they see and send their lists to scientists through a website or app. This event is called the Great Backyard Bird Count, and it is one of the largest citizen science projects in the world.\n\nThe count began in 1998 in North America. Scientists knew that birds are good signs of the health of the environment. If the number of a certain bird falls quickly, it often means that something is wrong with its habitat. However, scientists could not be everywhere at once. They needed help from the public. The idea was simple: if enough people counted birds at the same time, researchers would get a picture of where birds were and how many there were.\n\nYou do not need to be an expert to take part. Beginners can use free apps that help them recognise birds by their colour, size or song. Experienced birdwatchers check the lists, and the computer system warns if someone reports something very unlikely, such as a penguin in a city park. Because of these checks, the information is reliable enough to be used in real research.\n\nOver the years, the count has helped scientists notice important changes. For example, they have seen some species moving further north as winters become warmer. The project also has another benefit: many participants say it helps them relax and notice nature in places they pass every day without looking.",
